namespace DinoEditor.App.Projects;

/// <summary>
/// The answer to "will replacing this vanilla tile actually work?", asked once at import while the
/// install is loaded.
/// </summary>
/// <param name="ShippingPacks">
/// DLC packs that also ship the tile. INFORMATION, not the verdict - see the remarks.
/// </param>
/// <param name="UnreadablePacks">
/// Patchday packs that were found but could NOT be walked - a root or nested archive failed to
/// open. Non-empty means an EMPTY <paramref name="ShippingPacks"/> is not evidence of anything.
/// </param>
/// <remarks>
/// THE VERDICT IS <see cref="ShippedByBaseGame"/>, AND IT USED TO BE <paramref name="ShippingPacks"/>.
/// Six in-game runs on 27-28/08/2026 (docs/bench/E1-override-experiment.md) measured the rule as: a
/// stream resource overrides a tile the base game does NOT ship, and cannot override one it does -
/// whatever the file holds and whoever wrote it. Patchday was never the rule. The patchday tiles E1
/// tested were base-game tiles as well, which is why they failed, and gating on patchday was
/// therefore wrong in BOTH directions: it let 1,508 base-only tiles through, and it would have
/// refused a DLC-only tile that a pack happened to re-ship.
///
/// The pack lists stay because they are worth telling the mapper - they correlate with real DLC
/// behaviour and cost nothing to carry - but nothing refuses on them any more.
///
/// EVERY FIELD TRAVELS TOGETHER ON PURPOSE, and that is the whole point of this type existing
/// rather than the check returning a bare bool.
///
/// The gate fails OPEN. An archive that cannot be walked contributes no ymap names, so a base-game
/// tile inside it reads as overridable, so the override looks safe and the mapper ships a file the
/// engine throws away - the worst failure shape available, because it looks like success right up
/// until the map is not there in game.
///
/// FAILURE IS NOT SYMMETRIC. A POSITIVE <see cref="ShippedByBaseGame"/> is always trustworthy: the
/// name was actually seen in an x64 archive. A NEGATIVE may not be, which is what
/// <see cref="UnscannedArchives"/> is for. So the caller ALLOWS-AND-WARNS on an unverifiable
/// negative rather than refusing - a refusal the mapper cannot act on is worse than a warning they
/// can - and holding both in one value is what stops a future reader taking a false
/// ShippedByBaseGame as "safe" without also seeing whether the question could be answered at all.
/// </remarks>
public readonly record struct OverrideCheck(
    IReadOnlyList<string> ShippingPacks,
    IReadOnlyList<string> UnreadablePacks)
{
    private readonly IReadOnlyList<string>? _unscannedArchives;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the BASE GAME ships this tile, in which case no stream resource can override it.
    /// This is the refusal, and the only one.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// "SHIPPED BY BASE", NOT "SHIPPED ONLY BY BASE". The first version of the rule asked for
    /// base-and-nothing-else and the cross-check against the in-game runs caught it:
    /// <c>id2_21_e_strm_0</c> is BaseGame + patchday1ng + patchday2ng, so "base only" reads FALSE
    /// and would have permitted an export E1 measured as ignored in game. 2,885 of the 4,593
    /// base-game tiles are re-shipped by some dlcpack, so the distinction is not academic.
    /// </remarks>
    public bool ShippedByBaseGame { get; init; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Archives that were found but could NOT be walked end to end, so a FALSE
    /// <see cref="ShippedByBaseGame"/> is "not found", not "not shipped".
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Reads as empty on a <c>default</c> value rather than throwing, because a struct's field
    /// initializers do not run for <c>default(OverrideCheck)</c> and a null here would surface as a
    /// NullReferenceException inside the import path rather than as a missing warning.
    /// </remarks>
    public IReadOnlyList<string> UnscannedArchives
    {
        get => _unscannedArchives ?? Array.Empty<string>();
        init => _unscannedArchives = value;
    }

    /// <summary>The answer when nothing can be asked - no install, a headless run, or a test that
    /// does not exercise the vanilla path. Permissive and SILENT: no archive was found to be
    /// unreadable because none was looked for, and warning about an incomplete scan that never
    /// started would train the mapper to ignore the warning that matters.</summary>
    public static OverrideCheck Unknown { get; } =
        new(Array.Empty<string>(), Array.Empty<string>());
}

